---
// Astro returns BASE_URL without a trailing slash at the root but with one
// under a subpath; normalise so `${base}x` is never `/cyber-cvx`.
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import { recentWork } from '../data/hive';

/**
 * How this site was made, told honestly.
 *
 * The commit log says what changed. This says what happened — including the
 * defects found on the way, which are the part worth reading and the part a
 * commit message has no room for.
 */
const days = [
  {
    date: '14 August',
    head: 'From one HTML file to a system',
    body: `It started as a single 16 KB page with no dependencies. That was the wrong
      shape: four subpages and a credential gallery meant duplicated markup in every
      file. Rebuilt on Astro and Tailwind in an afternoon — the same stack as the
      projects it describes, which is the point.`,
    findings: [
      {
        head: 'The numbers had to stop being typed in',
        body: `A CV that claims 29,995 embedded points and cannot re-derive the figure
          is making a claim, not a measurement. So a script reads Postgres and Qdrant
          and rewrites the data file; a second mode fails the build when the committed
          numbers no longer match. Tested by deliberately breaking a figure — exit 1,
          as intended.`,
      },
      {
        head: 'Two independent reviewers, no hive context',
        body: `Asked for the flaws rather than the merits. The sharpest finding was
          measurable: philosophy and self-reference occupied 41.2% of the page text
          while systems and results held 14.9%. Inverted — evidence sections now hold
          63.9%. One reviewer also called an enthusiastic AI quote what it was, and it
          was cut.`,
      },
      {
        head: 'A defect in my own generator, of exactly the class this site is about',
        body: `The stats script carried historical figures forward by slicing the old
          file at a comment string. On a failed match, indexOf returns −1 and slice(−1)
          takes the last character — the file would have been overwritten with one byte
          and no error raised. Found by review, not by running it. That data now lives
          in its own module the script never opens.`,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    date: '17 August',
    head: 'A theme song, a crystal, and a rebuild of the whole front page',
    body: `The design went obsidian and neon, three.js arrived, and the site went
      public on GitHub Pages. Then the person whose CV it is pointed out that it had
      become a site about systems rather than about him — which was true, and the
      most useful correction of the project.`,
    findings: [
      {
        head: 'Serving it under a subpath found a bug nothing else would',
        body: `GitHub Pages serves from /cyber-cv/, so every absolute link needs the
          base prefix. Adding it produced /cyber-cvfavicon.svg — BASE_URL carries no
          trailing slash, so every concatenation was one character short. Only visible
          by serving the build under the real path locally before pushing.`,
      },
      {
        head: 'My own probe said the canvas was blank; the screenshot said otherwise',
        body: `A readPixels check on the 3D crystal reported zero lit pixels. A
          screenshot showed it rendering correctly — the probe read the buffer after
          the frame had been handed off. The instrument was wrong, not the page, which
          is the failure mode this whole site keeps returning to.`,
      },
      {
        head: 'One entity name contained a newline',
        body: `The crystal generator parses psql output line by line. A single graph
          entity with a line break in its name shifted every field after it and produced
          NaN inside a SQL IN clause. Fixed in the query rather than patched downstream,
          and guarded so a malformed row cannot reach the generated file.`,
      },
      {
        head: 'An HTML comment inside an Astro expression',
        body: `Broke the build with "CompilerError: Unexpected token" — no file, no
          line. Found by removing files one at a time until the error stayed. In a JSX
          expression the comment has to be JS syntax; the error message will not tell
          you that.`,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    date: '19 August',
    head: 'Two defects found by being told the result felt wrong',
    body: `The day the site was supposed to be finished, and the two most useful findings
      came from someone reading the output rather than from any check I ran. Both were in the
      research and the tooling behind it, not in the page.`,
    findings: [
      {
        head: 'The measured rule set was selected by a filename pattern',
        body: `Reading the published list of sixteen rules, Michal noticed the one he uses
          most was absent. It was: the study had been seeded from files matching rule-*.md,
          which selects rules that happen to have a certain filename rather than rules in use.
          The missing one has 115 memory files against 51 for the best-represented rule that
          was included, graph degree 38 against 8, and the set even contains a rule whose
          stored title names it as a sibling. Three more surfaced with the same key. Across
          every rule-shaped entity, the excluded ones average degree 7.1 against 3.9 — the
          selection was biased against the most established material. They are now listed as
          unmeasured rather than folded into the results.`,
      },
      {
        head: 'Search returns rank, not relevance',
        body: `Every result carries 1/(60 + rank) — 0.0164, 0.0161, 0.0159 — regardless of
          query or how relevant the hit is, because rank fusion uses position and discards the
          semantic score by design. Correct for merging incomparable lists; the consequence is
          that a layer returning four hits shows no difference between one real answer and
          three tangents.`,
      },
      {
        head: 'And it has no sense of time',
        body: `Asked what this project is, the local model answered from a two-day-old plan —
          a single-file page with no dependencies — while the current state is eleven Astro
          pages, live. Not missing data: the store holds both versions and nothing ranks the
          later one higher. The code comments claim the other layers carry no comparable
          timestamp; two of them do. An agent can therefore report a superseded state
          confidently, which is worse than returning nothing.`,
      },
      {
        head: 'Nothing about this project was in the hive at all',
        body: `Thirty commits and eleven pages, and the knowledge store held zero records
          about any of it. Lessons, papers and a thesis had been written throughout — never
          the state of the thing being built. It surfaced only because someone asked a
          question I would not have asked, which is the shape of every finding on this page:
          absence has nothing to search for.`,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    date: '30–31 August',
    head: 'A provenance layer, and eleven defects it found in us',
    body: `A ninth store went in: research sources, kept apart from the memory the system
      writes itself. The point was never retrieval — it was being able to tell a paper from a
      summary of one, six months later, when nobody remembers which was which. Everything
      below was measured on the real corpus, and almost all of it is a defect in our own code.`,
    findings: [
      {
        head: 'Ranking cannot tell a source from a retelling',
        body: `Asked for the minimum energy to erase one bit, the store returned four
          model-written syntheses and not Shannon's own paper, which was sitting in the same
          collection with 106 chunks. Both results are semantically reasonable — that is
          exactly the problem, because nothing in the scores says one is a source. Every chunk
          now carries a trust tier assigned at ingest from real evidence, and the search says
          out loud when everything it returned is unverified.`,
      },
      {
        head: 'Two of seven, then seven of seven',
        body: `An inventory before any question: of the thinkers our writing leans on, only
          Shannon, Carroll and Boltzmann were held in the original. Landauer, Maxwell, Cicero
          and Spinoza reached us only through retellings, so every claim attributed to them
          rested on one. The local model fetched ten open sources across two rounds — 10 of 10,
          each report accurate, including a correction to the list I had given it. Landauer's
          bound is now citable from the open experimental verification of it; the paywalled
          1961 original was left alone. Primary chunks went from 318 to 1,837.`,
      },
      {
        head: 'A classifier that called two peer-reviewed papers unverified',
        body: `Constitutional AI and Machine Psychology both arrived and were tiered as
          model-written, on one occurrence each in thirty pages: both use the phrase
          "AI-generated" — about their subject matter. Papers about AI-generated content,
          marked as AI-generated content. The rule this produced: a phrase a paper would
          naturally discuss cannot be a provenance marker; a marker must be something only a
          generator says about itself. That rule then decided two further cases correctly.`,
      },
      {
        head: 'Provenance and relevance fail separately',
        body: `Asked what the sources establish about Gödel's incompleteness theorem, the
          search returned three chunks of Lewis Carroll's Symbolic Logic at the highest trust
          tier — and the warning stayed silent, which reads as "here is a citable answer".
          Carroll died in 1898; Gödel published in 1931. A weak match wearing a good tier is
          the more dangerous case, because provenance is what makes a reader stop checking.`,
      },
      {
        head: 'The check that nearly recorded a false accusation',
        body: `Verifying a claim the local model had made about our sources, I counted zero
          occurrences of "Gödel" in the paper it cited and was one step from writing down that
          it had invented the reference. It had not. PDF extraction renders the name as
          "G¨odel" — a split diacritic — across sixty-two sites in two papers, author names
          included, so a search for the word silently fails to match the text containing it.
          The defect surfaced only because I checked a claim I expected to be false.`,
      },
      {
        head: 'A visualisation that cited telemetry it never read',
        body: `Among concept images generated elsewhere for this research, one plots this
          system's energy cost at 3.5× the Landauer limit. A single embedding call here costs
          about 8.5 × 10^15 times that bound — sixteen orders of magnitude out — and the chart's
          footer credits "Factorium Symbiosis v01 Telemetry", which does not exist; the surfaces
          are sine waves. The finding is not the inaccuracy. It is that a citation is itself a
          claim, and nothing in a rendered chart distinguishes a plotted measurement from a
          plotted assumption.`,
      },
      {
        head: 'Seven of the defects were ours, and none was found by introspection',
        body: `A coverage check comparing disk against index. A URL list verified before being
          handed over. One regular expression checked against another. The study set out to
          compare our retrieval against a well-funded alternative and spent most of its time
          finding faults on this side — which is the only outcome that would have made it worth
          recording. A study of provenance that finds no fault in itself has not looked.`,
      },
    ],
  },
];

// Derived, not written down: the header said "four defects" while the page listed
// eleven, because the prose and the data were maintained separately. A number that
// describes the content has to come FROM the content.
const findingCount = days.reduce((n, e) => n + e.findings.length, 0);

const stats = [
  { v: '58', l: 'commits' },
  { v: '11', l: 'pages' },
  { v: '~8', l: 'working days' },
  { v: '297', l: 'entries in the search index' },
];
---
<Base
  title="Build log — Michal Malček"
  description="How this site was made, including the defects found on the way."
  back={{ href: `${base}`, label: 'Back to overview' }}
>
  <header class="pt-10 pb-8 border-b border-line">
    <div class="eyebrow mb-3">How this was made</div>
    <h1 class="text-[clamp(33px,4.4vw,47px)] font-semibold tracking-tight m-0 mb-3">
      <span class="glow-name">Build log</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="text-fg-dim max-w-[680px] m-0">
      A site that argues for keeping an honest record should keep one about itself. The commit
      history says what changed; this says what happened — including the {findingCount} defects found
      along the way, which is the part a commit message has no room for.
    </p>
  </header>

  <Section title="In numbers">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-3">
      {stats.map((s, i) => (
        <div class="card p-5" style={`animation: countIn .5s ease-out ${i * 60}ms both`}>
          <div class="text-[31px] font-semibold lit-forge leading-none">{s.v}</div>
          <div class="text-[17px] text-fg-ghost mt-2">{s.l}</div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Section>

  {days.map((d) => (
    <Section title={d.date} note={d.head}>
      <p class="text-fg-dim max-w-[720px] mt-0 mb-4">{d.body}</p>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-3">
        {d.findings.map((f) => (
          <div class="card p-5">
            <b class="text-[17.5px] text-fg block mb-1.5 leading-snug">{f.head}</b>
            <p class="text-[17px] text-fg-dim m-0">{f.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </Section>
  ))}

  <Section title="The rule that produced every one of them" note="one habit">
    <div class="card p-5">
      <p class="text-[17.5px] text-fg-dim m-0 mb-3">
        Every defect above was found the same way: <b class="text-fg">by checking a thing whose
        answer was already known.</b> Serve the build under the real path. Screenshot the canvas
        rather than trusting the probe. Break a figure on purpose and confirm the gate fails.
        Not one of them came from a passing test.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-fg-ghost m-0">
        The same habit is what the research on this site measures, and the same habit is what my
        own error record says I break most often — 35 of 46 mistakes are a check that came back
        empty and got read as an answer. Knowing a rule and holding to it under load are two
        different things, which is the finding, not the excuse.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Recent commits, from the environment" note="read from git">
    <div class="card p-5">
      <p class="text-[17px] text-fg-dim mt-0 mb-4">
        Not this repository — these are the last twelve commits on Factorium, pulled from its git
        history at build time. Included because a commit log is the honest version of a CV: written
        while the work happens, by someone who does not yet know how it turns out.
      </p>
      <ol class="list-none p-0 m-0 flex flex-col">
        {recentWork.map((c) => (
          <li class="grid grid-cols-[86px_1fr] gap-3 py-[7px] border-b border-line/40 last:border-b-0 items-baseline">
            <span class="text-[17px] text-fg-ghost tabular-nums">{c.date}</span>
            <span class="text-[17px] text-fg-dim">{c.subject}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Base>
